REM ============================================================================
REM ED-MENUS.BASL -- the editor's menus, with their TEXT in banked RAM.
REM
REM #INCLUDEd inline by the editor exactly as ED-STORE.BASL is: one global symbol
REM table, so these labels are GOSUBbed from the main program and the MENU.* API
REM variables below are visible to both.
REM
REM WHAT THIS DEMONSTRATES
REM   A Blitz string variable OWNS a block on the string heap, that block is never
REM   handed back and never shrinks, and the heap shares one 25,600-byte pot with the
REM   compiled program itself. So every string a program HOLDS is low memory spent for
REM   the whole run, whether it is being looked at or not.
REM
REM   BANKING HELPS STRINGS YOU HOLD. IT CANNOT HELP STRINGS YOU USE. A string value
REM   is a bare 16-bit pointer with no bank byte in it, so anything about to go through
REM   LEN, MID$, PRINT or a comparison must be in low RAM at that moment. Menu text is
REM   the ideal case for the first: written once at startup, read only on the keystroke
REM   that opens a menu, and never touched by the render path at all.
REM
REM   The whole menu layer lives here so the technique reads end to end -- the banked
REM   table, the data going into it, and the two routines drawing out of it. EDITOR.BASL
REM   holds no menu text of its own.
REM
REM STORAGE LAYOUT -- FIXED STRIDE, AND DELIBERATELY NOT AN ALLOCATOR
REM   ED-STORE.BASL shows the other shape, a bump allocator with a pointer table, because
REM   a document line can be any length and there may be thousands. Menus are the
REM   opposite -- a small fixed set, known at write time -- so a slot is arithmetic and
REM   nothing else: slot N is at $A000 + N * EDM.STRIDE, holding [len][chars]. No table,
REM   no free list, no fragmentation, O(1) both ways. Pick the layout that matches what
REM   you are storing; a bump allocator here would be code earning nothing.
REM
REM     slot  0..31   item text, flat: menu * 8 + item
REM     slot 32..35   the accelerator letters, one string per menu
REM     slot 36..39   the menu titles
REM
REM EVERY ACCESSOR SETS ITS OWN BANK, which is the rule the rest of the editor already
REM follows -- see ED-STORE.BASL's DOC.SLOT.READ and STASH.INC.BL. PEEK and POKE restore
REM the bank around each access, so the last BANK statement wins, and a routine assuming
REM someone else left the right bank selected is a bug waiting for a reorder. ED.ALTKEYS
REM is the live example: it selects bank 0 to reach the KERNAL keymap, and it must not
REM call in here between doing so and its PEEKs.
REM
REM WHAT IT SAVES HERE, MEASURED, AND IT IS A WASH -- WHICH IS THE LESSON
REM   FRE(0) either side of ED.MENU.SETUP, release build, before and after the move:
REM
REM     low RAM taken by menu setup   473 -> 224 bytes   saved 249
REM     p-code                      12910 -> 13155       cost  245
REM     free at that point           5449 -> 5420        NET   -29
REM
REM   The 14 strings really were 249 bytes of low memory and really are gone. EDM.PUT,
REM   EDM.GET and the setup lines cost almost exactly the same again, so at this size the
REM   technique pays for itself and no more.
REM
REM   THE WIN SCALES WITH STRING LENGTH, NOT WITH COUNT. A block is max(10, len * 1.5) + 3,
REM   so a 4-character menu item costs 13 bytes and moving it saves about 6 net -- but a
REM   30-character one costs 48 and saves about 41. Menu items are short, which is why this
REM   example breaks even. Point the same 40 lines at help text, templates, a file list or
REM   anything else long and held, and it pays from the first few strings.
REM ============================================================================

REM Bank 5. 1..3 are ED-STORE's line table, 4 is lent to GUI.INC.BL for the cells a dialog
REM covers, and the document arena starts at 6 -- it only grows upward, so carving a bank
REM off the bottom is the cheapest reservation there is.
#DEFINE EDM.BANK 5
#DEFINE EDM.WIN 40960
#DEFINE EDM.STRIDE 32
#DEFINE EDM.ITEM 0
#DEFINE EDM.HOT 32
#DEFINE EDM.NAME 36

REM ---- write EDM.TEXT$ into slot EDM.SLOT ----
REM GP.STRPTR gives the address of the string's [ActLen][Data], so the characters start one
REM past it and the copy is a flat byte loop -- no MID$ per character, which would allocate
REM a one-character string for every byte stored. NEVER GP.STRPTR A LITERAL: that points
REM into the p-code, and writing through it edits the running program.
EDM.PUT:
  EDM.ADDR = EDM.WIN + EDM.SLOT * EDM.STRIDE
  EDM.LEN = LEN(EDM.TEXT$)
  IF EDM.LEN > EDM.STRIDE - 1 THEN EDM.LEN = EDM.STRIDE - 1
  EDM.PTR = GP.STRPTR(EDM.TEXT$)
  BANK EDM.BANK
  POKE EDM.ADDR, EDM.LEN
  REM FOR 1 TO 0 RUNS ONCE in this BASIC, so an empty string has to leave before the loop.
  IF EDM.LEN = 0 THEN RETURN
  REM The window is banked and the string is not: one BANK covers both halves of the copy,
  REM because low RAM does not care which bank is selected.
  FOR EDM.I = 1 TO EDM.LEN
    POKE EDM.ADDR + EDM.I, PEEK(EDM.PTR + EDM.I)
  NEXT EDM.I
  RETURN

REM ---- read slot EDM.SLOT back into EDM.TEXT$ ----
REM RPT$ makes a string of the right length in one allocation and the bytes are then
REM overwritten where they lie. Growing it a character at a time would be O(n^2) in
REM allocation AND would leave a dead block behind on every step.
EDM.GET:
  EDM.ADDR = EDM.WIN + EDM.SLOT * EDM.STRIDE
  BANK EDM.BANK
  EDM.LEN = PEEK(EDM.ADDR)
  EDM.TEXT$ = ""
  IF EDM.LEN = 0 THEN RETURN
  EDM.TEXT$ = RPT$(32, EDM.LEN)
  EDM.PTR = GP.STRPTR(EDM.TEXT$)
  FOR EDM.I = 1 TO EDM.LEN
    POKE EDM.PTR + EDM.I, PEEK(EDM.ADDR + EDM.I)
  NEXT EDM.I
  RETURN


REM ---- menu tables (data-driven) ----
REM The bar's layout is MENUBAR.INC.BL's: MENUBAR.WHERE works out a title's column from the
REM item text on demand, which is what ED.DRAW.DROPDOWN hangs the panel from. MENU.WIDTH
REM stays because it is the DROPDOWN's width, and a bar has no column width.
ED.MENU.SETUP:
  MENU.COUNT = 3
  DIM MENU.ITEMS(3) : DIM MENU.WIDTH(3) : REM numbers, so low RAM costs them nothing
  REM MENUVERT.ITEM$ IS THE ONE MENU ARRAY THAT CANNOT MOVE. MENUVERT reads it directly,
  REM and a string value is a bare pointer with no bank in it, so whatever the library is
  REM about to draw has to be in low RAM. It holds one menu's worth at a time; the other
  REM three arrays are gone, and their text lives in bank EDM.BANK.
  DIM MENUVERT.ITEM$(8)
  REM THE TEXT GOES STRAIGHT INTO THE BANK and is never held in a variable again. One
  REM line per string, because each is one EDM.PUT -- the table is the code.
  MENU.ITEMS(0) = 5 : MENU.ITEMS(1) = 2 : MENU.ITEMS(2) = 1
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.NAME +  0 : EDM.TEXT$ = "File"         : GOSUB EDM.PUT
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.ITEM +  0 : EDM.TEXT$ = "New"          : GOSUB EDM.PUT
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.ITEM +  1 : EDM.TEXT$ = "Open..."      : GOSUB EDM.PUT
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.ITEM +  2 : EDM.TEXT$ = "Save"         : GOSUB EDM.PUT
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.ITEM +  3 : EDM.TEXT$ = "Save As..."   : GOSUB EDM.PUT
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.ITEM +  4 : EDM.TEXT$ = "Exit"         : GOSUB EDM.PUT
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.NAME +  1 : EDM.TEXT$ = "Search"       : GOSUB EDM.PUT
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.ITEM +  8 : EDM.TEXT$ = "Find..."      : GOSUB EDM.PUT
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.ITEM +  9 : EDM.TEXT$ = "Go to Line...": GOSUB EDM.PUT
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.NAME +  2 : EDM.TEXT$ = "Help"         : GOSUB EDM.PUT
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.ITEM + 16 : EDM.TEXT$ = "About"        : GOSUB EDM.PUT
  REM STAYS IN LOW RAM, AND EARNS IT. It is three bytes, and it is now the ONLY place the
  REM menu accelerators are read from: the bar tints from it, and ED.ALTKEYS, ED.ALT.MENU
  REM and ED.MENU.LETTER all scan it rather than fetching a title back out of the bank --
  REM ED.ALTKEYS in particular has bank 0 selected for the keymap while it does so.
  MENU.HOT$ = "FSH"
  REM ONE LETTER PER ITEM, IN ITEM ORDER, and it does not have to be the initial: Save
  REM and Save As... both start with S, so the second takes its A. MENUVERT matches
  REM without case and tints the letter at a word start where it can find one -- the A
  REM of "As", not the a of "Save". A letter not in the item at all is still a working
  REM accelerator; it just does not light up.
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.HOT +  0 : EDM.TEXT$ = "NOSAX" : GOSUB EDM.PUT : REM New Open Save saveAs eXit
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.HOT +  1 : EDM.TEXT$ = "FG"    : GOSUB EDM.PUT : REM Find, Go to line
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.HOT +  2 : EDM.TEXT$ = "A"     : GOSUB EDM.PUT : REM About
  REM The widths are measured from the bank, once, and kept as NUMBERS -- which is the
  REM whole trick: what a banked string is asked for at startup need never be asked again.
  FOR MENU.I = 0 TO MENU.COUNT - 1
    EDM.SLOT = EDM.NAME + MENU.I : GOSUB EDM.GET
    MENU.W = LEN(EDM.TEXT$)
    FOR MENU.J = 0 TO MENU.ITEMS(MENU.I) - 1
      EDM.SLOT = EDM.ITEM + MENU.I * 8 + MENU.J : GOSUB EDM.GET
      IF LEN(EDM.TEXT$) > MENU.W THEN MENU.W = LEN(EDM.TEXT$)
    NEXT MENU.J
    MENU.WIDTH(MENU.I) = MENU.W + 2
  NEXT MENU.I
  RETURN


REM ---- the bar's items, into the array MENUBAR reads ----
REM MENUBAR and MENUVERT share MENUVERT.ITEM$ on purpose, so it holds whichever of the two
REM is being drawn and this routine and ED.DRAW.DROPDOWN each load their own first. Cheap;
REM keeping two copies in step would not be.
REM
REM AN ITEM IS " NAME " AND THE SPACES ARE THE LAYOUT -- MENUBAR makes each item exactly as
REM wide as its text, so the air around a title, and the highlight's margin, has to be in
REM the title itself.
ED.MENU.BAR.ITEMS:
  FOR MENU.I = 0 TO MENU.COUNT - 1
    EDM.SLOT = EDM.NAME + MENU.I : GOSUB EDM.GET
    MENUVERT.ITEM$(MENU.I + 1) = " " + EDM.TEXT$ + " "
  NEXT MENU.I
  MENUVERT.COUNT = MENU.COUNT : MENUVERT.X = 0 : MENUVERT.Y = 0
  MENUVERT.ATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.TITLE)
  MENUVERT.HIATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.HILITE)
  MENUVERT.HOT$ = MENU.HOT$
  MENUVERT.HOTATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.BORDER)
  MENUBAR.X = 0 : MENUBAR.Y = 0 : MENUBAR.GAP = 0
  RETURN


REM The accelerator letter's colour is MENUVERT.ROW's hotkey tint: MENU.HOT$ names the
REM letters and MENUVERT.HOTATTR is the colour, so no title is painted in two pieces here.



REM ---- dropdown (VERA rows 1..) ----
REM MENUVERT.ROW draws one row: GP.FILL for the background, GP.PRINTAT for the text. Both
REM need the ISO flag ED.INIT sets, or GP.PRINTAT converts ASCII as though it were PETSCII
REM and every letter comes out case-swapped.
REM
REM WHERE IT HANGS FROM IS MENUBAR'S ANSWER: MENUBAR.WHERE walks the item text left of
REM MENUBAR.SEL and leaves the column in MENUBAR.SELX. The panel starts one cell IN from
REM the item's left edge, which lines the item text up under the title text -- both are a
REM leading space wide.
REM
REM ED.MENU.BAR.ITEMS first, because MENUBAR.WHERE measures MENUVERT.ITEM$ and this routine
REM is about to fill that same array with the dropdown's items.
ED.DRAW.DROPDOWN:
  ED.DD.N = MENU.ITEMS(MENU.ACTIVE)
  GOSUB ED.MENU.BAR.ITEMS
  MENUBAR.SEL = MENU.ACTIVE + 1 : GOSUB MENUBAR.WHERE
  ED.DD.COL = MENUBAR.SELX + 1  : ED.DD.W = MENU.WIDTH(MENU.ACTIVE)

  ED.DD.BX = ED.DD.COL - 1 : IF ED.DD.BX < 0 THEN ED.DD.BX = 0
  REM EVERY Y HERE IS A MAP ROW, NOT A SCREEN ROW. The GP commands know nothing about the
  REM editor's hardware scroll -- they write at map row = whatever Y they are given -- so
  REM the offset is added here, or the panel lands ED.MAP.TOP rows from the bar it hangs
  REM from: invisible at the top of the document and further adrift the further you scroll.
  ED.DD.FW = ED.DD.W + 2 : ED.DD.FH = ED.DD.N + 2
  ED.DD.TY = 1 : ED.DD.BY = ED.DD.TY + ED.DD.FH - 1
  ED.DD.RX = ED.DD.BX + ED.DD.FW - 1 : ED.DD.FC = THEME.CLR(THEME.TEXT)
  REM GUI.FRAME, so the dropdown and the dialogs share one frame drawer -- and it is the
  REM only one that can draw a box here, since GP.BOX's built-in styles are built from
  REM screen codes this font no longer has. Setting GUI.BORDER does not disturb a later
  REM dialog: GUI.OPEN settles it from GUI.BORDER.IN on every call.
  GUI.LEFT = ED.DD.BX : GUI.TOP = ED.DD.TY 
  GUI.WIDTH = ED.DD.FW  : GUI.HEIGHT = ED.DD.FH : GUI.BORDER = ED.DD.FC
  GOSUB GUI.FRAME

  REM THE WHOLE INTERFACE, the way MENUDEMO.EXP.BL drives it -- not three fields and a
  REM hand-rolled loop over MENUVERT.ROW. ROW reads MENUVERT.ATTR itself, so a caller that
  REM leaves COUNT, ATTR and HIATTR at zero is guessing at the routine rather than calling
  REM it. Set the documented inputs and let MENUVERT.DRAW walk the rows.
  MENUVERT.X = ED.DD.COL
  MENUVERT.Y = 2 : REM layer 1, a screen row
  MENUVERT.WIDTH = ED.DD.W : MENUVERT.COUNT = ED.DD.N
  REM The DOCUMENT's own attribute, not a page colour of its own, so the panel reads as a
  REM framed hole in the document rather than a card laid on it. Only the selected row
  REM breaks it, which is the point of a highlight bar.
  MENUVERT.ATTR    = THEME.CLR(THEME.TEXT) : MENUVERT.HIATTR  = THEME.CLR(THEME.TITLE)
  REM THE ACCELERATOR LETTERS BELONG TO THE OPEN MENU'S ITEMS, and they must be set BEFORE
  REM the rows are drawn -- MENUVERT.ROW tints from MENUVERT.HOT$ as it prints each row, so
  REM setting them at the call to RUN is one draw too late and tints nothing.
  EDM.SLOT = EDM.HOT + MENU.ACTIVE : GOSUB EDM.GET
  MENUVERT.HOT$ = EDM.TEXT$
  REM BORDER's FOREGROUND over the ROW's OWN BACKGROUND. Taking the whole attribute would
  REM paint one cell of the bar's blue into a black row -- a gap in the row, not a hotkey.
  MENUVERT.HOTATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.TEXT) - MOD(THEME.CLR(THEME.TEXT), 16) + MOD(THEME.CLR(THEME.BORDER), 16)
  REM MENUVERT.ITEM$ is 1 based, MENU.SEL 0 based off the flat item slots. Converted once
  REM here rather than at each use.
  REM
  REM THIS IS THE ONLY PLACE THE ITEM TEXT IS EVER FETCHED, and it happens on the keystroke
  REM that opens the menu -- a few dozen PEEKs, against a panel draw. Hold, do not use.
  FOR ED.DD.I = 1 TO ED.DD.N
    EDM.SLOT = EDM.ITEM + MENU.ACTIVE * 8 + ED.DD.I - 1 : GOSUB EDM.GET
    MENUVERT.ITEM$(ED.DD.I) = " " + EDM.TEXT$
  NEXT ED.DD.I
  GOSUB MENUVERT.DRAW : MENUVERT.DRAWROW = MENU.SEL + 1 : MENUVERT.DRAWATTR = MENUVERT.HIATTR
  GOSUB MENUVERT.ROW
  RETURN

